Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Your back is getting stronger — keep building on this great week.",
  "summary": "This was a strong period for your primary goal of building a bigger back. You dedicated over half your total training volume to back work, and it paid off with a meaningful strength index jump from 106 to 110 — the only muscle group to show any progression this week. At 28 years old with a healthy BMI of 23.5, your body is well-positioned to handle and adapt to this kind of focused loading. Total volume of 28 sets and nearly 10,800 kg of tonnage is solid for a week's work. The main area to keep an eye on is the relative underrepresentation of chest, shoulders, and legs, which received very little attention compared to back and biceps.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "status": "on_track",
    "notes": "Back strength index rose from 106 to 110 this period, a clear and meaningful gain that directly supports the long-term goal of building a bigger back. Biceps held steady at 104, chest held at 100, shoulders at 101, and legs at 103 — no regression in any group, but also no gains outside of back. This is consistent with the volume distribution, where back received 55.6% of all sets. The previous period's note to maintain current progression is being honored on the main lift. The challenge going forward is finding ways to keep back progressing while also nudging the other muscle groups forward."
  },
